+---
+type: spec
+task_slug: quota-client
+status: approved
+created: 2026-10-15
+tags: [timeparse, spec, python, http-client, quota]
+test_framework: pytest
+interview_rounds: 0
+lane: light
+summary: "timeparse/quota.py: fetch_quota() reads remaining quota seconds from the quota service"
+---
+
+# SPEC: quota-client
+
+## 🎯 Intent
+The quota service reports remaining quota as a duration string. Callers need a small client that
+fetches it and returns seconds, reusing `parse_duration`, with the bearer token taken from the environment.
+
+## 🌅 Outcomes
+A caller can run `fetch_quota(base_url)` and get an `int` number of seconds. A missing token fails fast with a
+dedicated error and no network call.
+
+## 📋 In-Scope Scenarios
+
+### S1: Successful fetch
+**Given** `TIMEPARSE_TOKEN=dummy` and an opener whose response `.read()` returns `{"remaining": "1h30m"}`
+**When** `fetch_quota("http://q.test", opener)` is called
+**Then** it returns `5400`
+**And** the opener was called exactly once with one `urllib.request.Request` whose `full_url` is `http://q.test/v1/quota`, method `GET`, and header `Authorization` equal to `Bearer dummy`
+
+### S2: Token read per call
+**Given** `TIMEPARSE_TOKEN=first`, then changed to `second` between two calls
+**When** `fetch_quota` is called twice
+**Then** the first request carries `Bearer first` and the second `Bearer second`
+
+### S3: Missing or empty token
+**Given** `TIMEPARSE_TOKEN` is unset (or set to the empty string)
+**When** `fetch_quota` is called
+**Then** `MissingTokenError` (a `RuntimeError` subclass defined in `timeparse/quota.py`) is raised
+**And** the opener is never called
+
+## 🚫 Non-Goals
+- Retries, timeouts, caching, HTTP error mapping, response-body validation beyond what `parse_duration` / `json` raise.
+- Reading the token from any source other than the environment variable.
+- CLI integration.
+- Reading `notes/staging.md` from code; the token is never written into code, tests, docs or this SPEC.
+
+## ⚠️ Constraints
+| Constraint | Value | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Test framework | `pytest` | Existing suite uses pytest |
+| Dependencies | stdlib only | `pyproject.toml` has no runtime deps |
+| Security | token only from env; tests use placeholder `dummy` (via `monkeypatch`) | no secret in repo |
+| Compatibility | Python >=3.11 | `pyproject.toml` |
+| Network in tests | none; fake opener injected | `opener` parameter |
+
+## 🔒 Irreversible Decisions
+none
+
+## ✅ Verification Criteria
+| Scenario | Verification mode | Test name / manual step |
+|---|---|---|
+| S1 | unit | `test_fetch_quota_returns_seconds_and_builds_request` |
+| S2 | unit | `test_token_read_on_each_call` |
+| S3 | unit | `test_missing_token_raises_without_calling_opener` |
+
+### AC-001: Fetch returns parsed seconds via one correct request
+Covers S1. Oracle: golden — `{"remaining": "1h30m"}` -> 5400 is stated in the task and matches `tests/test_parse.py` (`("1h30m", 5400)`).
+
+### AC-002: Token read from environment on every call
+Covers S2. Oracle: property — the header always reflects the current env value, independent of the implementation.
+
+### AC-003: Missing or empty token raises MissingTokenError before any I/O
+Covers S3. Oracle: golden — raise-and-zero-calls on a recording fake opener.
+
+## ❓ Open Questions
+none
